text:
{
  "errors": [
    {
      "span": "Om, by the distinction of attributes and by the denial of difference, neither of these two is [the ultimate reality].",
      "category": "accuracy",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "span": "From the attributes in the statement, “He who is omniscient, all-knowing, whose austerity is made of knowledge,” Nature (Prakriti) is not [the ultimate reality].",
      "category": "accuracy",
      "severity": "critical"
    },
    {
      "span": "From the denial of difference in the statement, “Therefore, from this Brahman, name, form, and food are born,” Virinca is not [the ultimate reality].",
      "category": "terminology",
      "severity": "critical"
    },
    {
      "span": "Virinca",
      "category": "terminology",
      "severity": "critical"
    },
    {
      "span": "the phrase “beyond the Imperishable, the Supreme” is also merely an attribute.",
      "category": "accuracy",
      "severity": "major"
    },
    {
      "span": "Hari alone is considered to be the triad of Imperishables, possessing all qualities",
      "category": "fluency",
      "severity": "minor"
    },
    {
      "span": "even the one who has attained the status of Ishana is not Rudra.",
      "category": "accuracy",
      "severity": "critical"
    },
    {
      "span": "the translation omits the Sanskrit phrase “अपरं त्वक्षरं या सा प्रकृतिर्जडरूपिका।  श्रीः परा प्रकृतिः प्रोक्ता चेतना विष्णुसंश्रया।  तामक्षरं परं प्राहुः परतः परमक्षरम्।  हरिमेवाखिलगुणमक्षरत्रयमीरितम्॥”",
      "category": "omission",
      "severity": "critical"
    },
    {
      "span": "the translation adds “the denial of difference” which is not present in the original “भेदव्यपदेशाभ्यां”.",
      "category": "addition",
      "severity": "major"
    }
  ]
}
